\section{Starting points}{
The first candidate is always the unperturbed starting point, so the original
fit is always represented in the comparison.  Every other candidate perturbs
the unfixed population parameters on the scale the model is estimated on
(which for a mu-referenced parameter is usually the log scale), and clips the
result to the parameter's declared bounds.  Between-subject variances are
perturbed multiplicatively so they stay positive, and the resulting matrix is
made positive-definite with \code{\link[lotri:lotriNearPD]{lotri::lotriNearPD()}}.
}

\section{Screening}{
Fully estimating every candidate is wasteful when many of them start far from
anywhere sensible.  With the default \code{screen = "posthoc"} each candidate is
first evaluated with an empirical-Bayes step only, which costs a small
fraction of a full estimation, and only the best \code{nFit} candidates are then
fully estimated.
}

\section{Parallel estimation}{
Each estimation already runs across every available thread, so estimating
several starts at once oversubscribes the machine unless the thread budget is
divided.  \code{multistartControl(cores=)} therefore restricts each worker to a
single thread.  Whether that is faster than the serial default depends
entirely on the model; a model whose subjects parallelise well is usually
better off left serial.  Parallel estimation uses \code{\link[parallel:mclapply]{parallel::mclapply()}} and
is not available on Windows.
}

\section{Resuming}{
Each start is cached to \code{cacheDir} as it completes, so an interrupted run
resumes where it left off.  Increasing \code{n} on a later call re-uses the starts
already estimated and only estimates the new ones.  Pass \code{restart = TRUE} to
discard the cache, or \code{cacheDir = NA} to never write one.

Changing anything that alters what a starting point \emph{is} (\code{sampling},
\code{spread}, \code{which}, \code{perturbOmega}, \code{omegaFold} or \code{seed}) gives the run its
own cache, so a cached estimation is never re-used for a start it did not
come from.  A Latin hypercube is a design over all \code{n} candidates at once, so
growing an \code{"lhs"} run moves its earlier starting points; those starts are
detected and re-estimated rather than reported against the wrong starting
point.
}

